# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of the machine that
# cloned it. Without this file the answer depends entirely on each
# contributor's `core.autocrlf`, and the Git-for-Windows installer's default
# for that is `true` -- which checks every file out as CRLF and then fights
# the `mixed-line-ending` pre-commit hook on every single commit. `eol=lf`
# overrides core.autocrlf in both directions: LF in the index, LF in the
# working tree, everywhere.
#
# This normalises nothing on introduction: no tracked file held a CR byte
# when it was added (verified with `git grep -I --cached -l $'\r'`).
* text=auto eol=lf

# inst/extdata holds shipped TEST FIXTURES that some tests read back
# byte-wise, and in a delimited file whitespace is data rather than
# formatting -- the same reasoning that excludes this directory from the
# whitespace hooks in .pre-commit-config.yaml. `-text` tells git to leave
# these bytes exactly as they are, in both directions. Diffs still work;
# only end-of-line conversion is switched off.
inst/extdata/** -text

# Explicit rather than relying on git's content heuristic, so a fixture that
# happens to start with printable bytes can never be mistaken for text and
# corrupted by conversion.
*.png  binary
*.gz   binary
*.docx binary
